# Python-generated files
__pycache__/
*.py[oc]
build/
dist/
wheels/
*.egg-info

# Virtual environments
.venv

# Generated protobuf stubs — regenerated from contracts/scheduling.proto
*_pb2.py
*_pb2.pyi
*_pb2_grpc.py

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Measurement output. None of this is committed: traces are regenerable from
# (config, seed) byte-for-byte at the recorded generator sha (C-2), and logs are
# large and per-run. What IS committed is the run manifest (C-6), which carries the
# seed, the trace sha256, the generator sha, and the git shas needed to reproduce
# any of it (F-20).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
traces/
runs/**
results/
*.parquet
figures/

# Two exceptions, and both are things another person has to be able to read.
#
# The run manifest is the committed record of a run (F-20) — it carries the seed, the
# trace sha256 and the git shas needed to reproduce everything else. Week 3's validation
# anchors are *identified* by their manifests, so a checkout with no manifests has no
# anchor set and F-23 cannot be checked by anyone but me.
!runs/**/
!runs/**/manifest.json
!runs/**/README.md

# The engine benchmarks are the evidence for G1: whether the slow node's prefill is a
# property of the card or of how its engine was built. Small JSON, and the argument does not
# survive without them.
!runs/bench/*.json

# The two cost-model derivations results.md quotes: phase R by workload, and the same with
# intervals. Small, and both are regenerated whenever a class is recalibrated, so tracking
# them is how a stale figure gets noticed.
!runs/exp/cell_intervals_*.json
!runs/exp/phase_ratio_*.json
# The G4 evidence: what the simulator got right and wrong on the held-out shape runs.
!runs/exp/*/contrast_check.json
!runs/exp/*/contrast_check.md

# Test-plan 3.8's parity evidence: every decision the live scheduler and the simulator
# both made on one state, and whether they chose the same node. Small JSON, and it is
# the only record that the two vehicles share a decision rule, since the logs it reads
# are not committed.
!runs/exp/*/parity_check.json
!runs/exp/*/parity_check.md

# Week 3's two determinations are small JSON and they ARE the deliverable — an admissible
# set that lives only on my disk has not been reported alongside results (F-13), and a load
# band nobody else can read is not a characterization.
!runs/admissible/*.json
!runs/**/load_band.json

# And the TIME-ORDERED C-3 snapshot series, which is an input to B's DES and to H3.
# Versioned under contracts/cost_models/<node_class>/ so that parameterizing the simulator
# does not require a copy of my machine.
!contracts/examples/*.json

# UML figures: sources and the report's pdf-notitle set are committed; the PNG and
# title-bearing PDF renders are ~6 MB and regenerable via docs/uml/render.sh.
docs/uml/out/png/
docs/uml/out/pdf/
docs/uml/.plantuml/

# Text extractions of the spec PDF. The .docx is edited off-machine and the PDF is the
# single authority; a committed text mirror went stale inside one cycle the last time.
docs/spec_extracted.txt
docs/*_extracted.txt

# protoc output — regenerated from contracts/scheduling.proto on import
dataplane/src/dataplane/_generated/

# trash
trash/

# The per-run-set summary, because every number in docs/results.md cites it and the
# parquet it is derived from is not committed. A cited number nobody else can open is
# not evidence.
!runs/**/summary.json

# coverage.py data from a pytest run at the repository root
.coverage
.coverage.*

# Throwaway local files. `demo` is the runbook I read off while driving the live
# demonstration, `generate_manifest.py` is the one-off that built that demonstration's
# manifest, `setup_usb.sh` built the stick for the CPU-only box, and `survey-*.json` is
# what tools/survey.sh printed about the machine it ran on. None of them is part of the
# instrument, so none of them is tracked.
/demo
/generate_manifest.py
/setup_usb.sh
/survey-*.json
/runs/demonstrate_jsq/
